---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
import * as CH from "./inflation-targeting.charts.js";
const NUM = CH.NUM;
---
<Learn
  title="The band is law, not decoration · India Atlas"
  description="Inflation targeting: since 2016 the RBI must hold CPI inflation at 4 per cent inside a 2–6 per cent band. Three straight quarters outside it, and the RBI must write to the government explaining itself."
  slug="inflation-targeting"
  kicker="Prices: Inflation targeting"
  h1="The band is <i>law, not decoration.</i>"
  lede="Since 2016 the Reserve Bank of India has had one job with a number attached: hold inflation at 4 per cent, inside a band from 2 to 6. What the band means, what happens when it breaks, and what it cannot do."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Inflation targeting"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Headline CPI vs the band, 2016–2025"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO, RBI"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "7 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Inflation targeting"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>In 2016, Parliament rewrote the Reserve Bank of India's job description. Price stability stopped being one goal among several and became the goal, with a number attached: keep consumer price inflation at 4 per cent, inside a band that runs from 2 to 6 per cent. A political promise was rewritten as arithmetic.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Inflation targeting <span class="sub">the RBI's mandate, since 2016</span></div>
          <p>A framework in which the central bank must aim at a publicly stated inflation number, with interest rates as its main tool. India's target is 4 per cent year-on-year CPI inflation, set by the government in consultation with the RBI and reviewed every five years.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the central bank's exam question, set in law, with the answer sheet published every month.</div>
        </aside>
        <aside class="defbox" style="margin-top:20px">
          <div class="term">Tolerance band <span class="sub">2% to 6%, the margin around the target</span></div>
          <p>The range around the target within which inflation may move without consequence. If the rate averages above 6 per cent or below 2 per cent for three consecutive quarters, the RBI must write to the government explaining why it missed, what it plans to do, and by when it expects to be back inside.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the band is not a forecast of where inflation will go; it is the fence around where inflation is allowed to stay.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">Notice what the band admits. Nobody can hold inflation at exactly 4 per cent in a country where nearly half the basket is food and the weather sets food prices. The band is an honest margin around an honest target: wide enough for the monsoon, narrow enough that missing it means something.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>The band is law.</b> The target sits in an amendment to the RBI Act of 1934, not in a press release.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>The penalty is a letter.</b> Miss for three straight quarters and the RBI must explain itself to the government in writing. It happened once in this decade, in 2022.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Both edges bind.</b> Too high fails the test. Too low fails it too.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : ten years against the band -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Ten years against the band</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Headline y/y %, monthly</span></div>
    <p>Below is the decade the framework has governed: the inflation rate drawn against its own rules, with the tolerance band shaded and the 4 per cent target dotted. The four marked excursions are the whole history of the band being tested.</p>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-band" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Mostly inside, four times out: the band doing its job</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI combined, year-on-year, shaded: tolerance 2–6%, dotted: 4% target, Jan 2016 to Dec 2025</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-it" set:html={NUM["pv-it"]}></div><div class="c">Dec 2025, the print</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-band" set:html={CH.band()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI combined, base 2012=100, vintage <span id="pf-it" set:html={NUM["pf-it"]}></span>, band and target: RBI Act amendment, 2016</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The shaded strip is the band; the dotted line is the target. The rate spends most of the decade inside the strip, which is the point of the framework: the band holds almost all of ordinary economic weather. The marked excursions are the exceptions. Twice food pushed the rate through the ceiling, once war and oil did, and once a year of falling food prices pulled it through the floor.</p>
            <p>Of the 120 months on this chart, <b id="it-m-above" set:html={NUM["it-m-above"]}></b> closed above the ceiling and <b id="it-m-below" set:html={NUM["it-m-below"]}></b> below the floor. The peaks: <b id="it-pk20" set:html={NUM["it-pk20"]}></b> in January 2020, <b id="it-pk22" set:html={NUM["it-pk22"]}></b> in April 2022 and <b id="it-pk23" set:html={NUM["it-pk23"]}></b> in July 2023. The floor break came in October 2025, at <b id="it-low25" set:html={NUM["it-low25"]}></b>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2022 case</span>
            <p>From January to September 2022 the rate never came back inside: quarterly averages of <b id="it-c-q1" set:html={NUM["it-c-q1"]}></b>, <b id="it-c-q2" set:html={NUM["it-c-q2"]}></b> and <b id="it-c-q3" set:html={NUM["it-c-q3"]}></b>, against a ceiling of 6 per cent. Three consecutive quarters outside the band triggered the clause, and in November 2022 the RBI wrote to the government explaining the miss.</p>
            <p>The letter is the framework working exactly as written. Not a scandal: a mechanism.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 what the framework changes -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>A promise with a denominator</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">What the band can and cannot do</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>An inflation target does something subtler than restrain the central bank. It disciplines everyone else. Budgets, wage settlements and administered prices are all written in the knowledge that beyond 6 per cent, someone must explain themselves in writing. The band converts a vague promise, low inflation, into a test that either passes or fails each quarter.</p>
        <p>What the target cannot do is set the price of onions. The RBI's tools move demand: credit, borrowing, spending. A food supply shock passes through the band on its own schedule, and the framework knows it, which is why the test is three consecutive quarters and not one bad month. The companion entry on <a href="/learn/monsoon-and-food-prices/">the monsoon and food prices</a> shows where those shocks come from.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iv</span>
          <b>Three quarters, not one month.</b> The test is slow on purpose: long enough that weather can pass through, short enough that drift cannot hide.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The band is a forecast.”</h3>
          <p>No. The band says nothing about where inflation will go; it says where inflation is allowed to be. A forecast can be wrong. The band is a rule, and a rule is either kept or breached.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Below the floor is always good news.”</h3>
          <p>It is cheap news with a catch. Inflation under 2 per cent is a breach of the mandate, not a bonus: sustained prints that low usually mean demand is weak, not merely that food is cheap. The floor exists because too little inflation is also a failure.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The RBI controls vegetable prices.”</h3>
          <p>It does not, and the framework knows it. Interest rates move demand, not the monsoon. The RBI can lean against a food spike spreading into wages and other prices; the spike itself belongs to the weather and the harvest.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Consumer Price Index group indices on base 2012=100, rural, urban and combined; retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04. The year-on-year rate is computed from the published combined index.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>The mandate: 4 per cent year-on-year CPI inflation within a 2–6 per cent tolerance band, in force since 2016 under the amended RBI Act. The statutory test uses quarterly averages; the chart shows monthly closes for texture.</p>
    <p>Where the data cannot answer a question, this page says so rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Prices, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. The 2022 letter is used as history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/cpi/">Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
